<!-- Alvik perceive-act body: the robot as the v3 socket body client, over a plain
     `ws://` link. This is the firmware's baked default scene (BEET_DEFAULT_SCENE), so
     a powered device boots straight into it. The composable transport (<AgentSocket>,
     connecting back to the perceive-act agent's socket server) wraps the individual
     capability routes it serves - `whoami` announces the `body` role for the agent's
     handshake, `drive` runs the wheels at the commanded differential velocity for a
     chosen duration. The transport and each capability are distinct, visible pieces,
     not a monolithic body element. The agent forwards each `drive` tool call over the
     socket; the same DriveForDuration command drives the wgpu fox and this robot.

     drive replies with the command's settle duration and the agent waits it out, so the
     next photo is only taken once the robot has stopped moving. The velocity (linear
     mm/s, angular deg/s) and duration (eg "1.5s") are chosen by the model per command
     and carried on the request, so there is no static tuning.

     The transport reconnects on its own with exponential backoff (250ms doubling to a
     10s ceiling), so this scene can be pushed before the agent is up, and survives the
     agent restarting, without being re-pushed. Losing the connection triggers a
     ResetScene (motors, wheels and LEDs to rest) before the redial loop takes over, so
     the robot never keeps driving a stale command while its agent is gone.

     Plain `ws` means no TLS or cert pinning, so the host's dev cert can regenerate
     freely without ever touching this link: the reliable default. The agent's socket
     server serves both schemes on one port, so the browser head keeps its `wss` (its
     webcam needs a secure context) while this body stays plaintext; drive commands are
     unauthenticated on the LAN, which is fine for a local robot. For an encrypted link
     (the robot pinning beet's dev public key) use the `perceive-act-body-wss.bsx`
     sibling instead.

     Start the perceive-act agent (bound to all interfaces), push with
     `beet load templates/alvik/perceive-act-body.bsx` (BEET_REMOTE_URL set to the
     device). Set `url` to the agent's host; omitting it falls back to the firmware's
     BEET_SOCKET_SERVER build env. -->
<AgentSocket url="ws://192.168.86.221:8338">
	<!-- a plain-ws body needs only these; no `secure` feature -->
	<CrateCheck features="beet_esp/alvik,beet_esp/sockets"/>
	<Route path="whoami" {WhoAmi}/>
	<Route path="drive" {DriveForDurationAction}/>
</AgentSocket>
